# `.Produces<object>` ratchet. MAY ONLY SHRINK.
#
# Each line is `<path>|<count>`: a file, and how many endpoints in it declare `.Produces<object>`
# and therefore name NO response type. A named type is the only thing a static check can compare a
# client DTO against, so every count here is response shape that nothing in this repo can see.
#
# This is not a style list. GET /api/registers/ was one of these, and RegisterSummaryInfo had
# drifted from it in two ways at once — a `string` against an enum the service writes as `1`, and a
# TenantId the server has never sent. Both silent; two consumers reported "0 registers" against a
# node holding five, for weeks, every gate green (#1613). Typing that one endpoint is what made it
# visible: with a real `.Produces<T>` the shape gate FAILS on it, and with `.Produces<object>` the
# gate goes blind and PASSES with the identical defect present.
#
# TO REMOVE OR LOWER A LINE — the only correct direction — declare the type the handler already
# returns. It is metadata only: no behaviour change, no wire change, no edit to the handler.
#
#     .Produces<object>(StatusCodes.Status200OK)
#  -> .Produces<IEnumerable<Sorcha.Register.Models.Register>>(StatusCodes.Status200OK)
#
# Then lower the count here in the SAME PR. A mismatch fails in both directions: a higher real count
# is a regression, a lower one is a stale entry that must be tightened, because a count left too
# high silently buys back room for a regression.
#
# NEVER raise a count or add a file to make a build pass.
#
# Regenerate the current numbers with:
#   pwsh scripts/check-produces-object.ps1 -Report
#
# Seeded 2026-09-08 at 93 across 19 files. Tenant Service is deliberately absent: it has 0 of its
# 161 `.Produces<T>` untyped, which is the proof this is reachable rather than aspirational.

